= Conversion at a type <sec-n3t>

_Written by `theory7`._ N3 at a type: two type values the checker identifies mean the same set. It is the part of N3 that N1 serves, since a type's meaning is read through the data model. The Lean proof for values that hold no code (`rules-trial`) took two facts of the data layer as hypotheses: that the library's `And`, `True` and `False` mean conjunction, truth and falsity, and that the unit laws keep typing as well as meaning (the plan, "What the two halves leave"). Both follow from N1 here.

*Type values.* A sort; an applied declaration $ty("D")(overline(v))$; $\&T$; an equation as `eq` builds it (`True`, `False`, a conjunction of equations, or a stuck `Eq`); a Π-closure; a neutral whose type is a sort (an unknown, or a sealed program whose head's codomain is a sort).

*Theorem N3t.* Let $v equiv w$ be type values at a state the checker reaches, both well typed inside at one sort. Then $v$ and $w$ mean the same set under every assignment. By induction on the derivation of $v equiv w$, which is finite (the relation is the least one, @sec-n3, CV7); one lemma per rule.

